[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWMC-183
TITLE: PC Ethernet Communication
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware communicates with the PC through an Ethernet interface using an RMII
transceiver and the LWIP UDP stack. Application traffic is separated into:

- Commands and their responses
- Unsolicited 100 Hz system-telemetry packets
- Unsolicited 1 Hz device-information packets
- Unsolicited fault-entry and fault-clear notifications

The PC can also request telemetry, device information, and an indexed fault entry. UDP is
used because timely delivery is more important than retransmitting stale real-time data.
Application payloads use the PC-Firmware Communication Protocol framing and CRC described
in the appendix.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-84
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-83
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-306
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWMC-184
TITLE: Peltier Cooler Communication
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware communicates with the external Peltier controller via one of the microcontroller's serial UART interfaces. The firmware both receives and transmits data on this UART port. Due to the nature of UART having independent transmit and receive lines, the transmit and receive functionality operate independently. As the UART baud rate is relatively slow compared to the processing speed of the microcontroller, both sending and receiving data is performed with nonblocking interrupt callback functions.

Transmitted data is sent as a buffer of bytes, representing a singular data packet to the PC, with the interrupt callback occurring once all data bytes have been transmitted. The firmware sets a flag when transmission is initiated, and clears the flag after transmission is finished, to not create transmission conflicts on the transmit line.

Reception is performed one byte at a time, with the interrupt callback occurring once a data byte is available. The parsing of the received byte is done to determine whether a complete data packet has been received from the PC, as according to the communication protocol. Once a completed data packet has been received, the firmware sets a flag for further data processing outside of the parsing inside of the interrupt callback.

The communication protocol is based on the pre-defined protocol defined by the off-the-shelf controller TC-48-20-OEM.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-64
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWMC-185
TITLE: Fault Handling
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware maintains a latched table of at most four active fault records. A record
contains the fault category, CRC-32 hash of the format string, clear epoch, entry index,
active count, captured system state, captured runtime, format-argument count, a
NUL-terminated 128-byte printable ASCII format string, and up to five 32-bit arguments.
Format specifiers identify unsigned integer, hexadecimal, floating-point, and literal
percent values so that the CNC can render a human-readable message.

Repeated reports with the same category and format are deduplicated while latched. A new
distinct record is published without waiting for a request. The PC retrieves a coherent
snapshot by requesting entries by index and checking the clear epoch and active count.
An out-of-range index returns an empty entry with the current snapshot metadata.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-6
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWMC-186
TITLE: PC Ethernet Data
STATEMENT: >>>
Each PC Ethernet communication packet contains a synchronization header, packet type,
packet counter, payload length, payload, and CRC footer. PC commands receive corresponding
responses. System telemetry, device information, new fault entries, and fault-clear
updates can also be transmitted without a preceding request. Packet types and payload
schemas are documented in the appendix section PC-Firmware Communication Protocol.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-46
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-84
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWMC-187
TITLE: State Machine
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware utilizes a state machine to perform the steps required for emission and execution of a treatment plan. The state machine relies on a state variable to keep track of the current state and utilizes 'events' to transition between states. The 'events' can be triggered by a variety of occurrences in the system, most often commands from the PC, stabilization of values from the HVPS, and well as any detected faults. The execution of functionality of the states is performed by calling on functions from an array of functions. The value of the state is used as the index to determine which function in the array to execute. Additional details on the state machine can be seen in the state machine diagram, as well as in the sections pertaining to each individual state.
<<<
RELATIONS:
