[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWMC-209
TITLE: Backup Timer Data
STATEMENT: >>>
The communication data sent by the main control application firmware are commands which are timer related tasks. These commands are setting the target time, pausing the timer counting and resetting the timer (including the interlock IO used to terminate emissions), as well as a report time request. There is no start command, as start is performed via hardware IO. This is due to the two timer MCUs sharing a single serial communication bus. To ensure that the timer counts are as synchronized as possible, the trigger to start is instead done via a digital signal rather than sequential serial commands. This signal only starts with the count of the timers and cannot stop them.

The communication data from the timers are the reported time value being counted, as well as the state of the timer.

Both received and sent messages use a simple checksum value to check the validity of the message payloads.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-10
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-53
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWMC-210
TITLE: Interlock Monitoring
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware samples and publishes every physical interlock input without applying the
fault mask to the reported status. It derives a separate required-interlock mask from the
compiled firmware mode and current state, publishes that mask in system telemetry, and
compares only those required bits with the sampled input status. A mismatch records an
interlock fault and initiates the existing latched-fault transition. Inputs omitted from
the required mask remain visible for diagnostics but do not independently create an
interlock fault in that state.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-8
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-25
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWMC-294
TITLE: Head Interface Board Data
STATEMENT: >>>
The transmitted data to the head interface board is the desired indicator LED output, representing the current state of the system. As this is a simple single byte value, it is sent multiple times in a single data packet, to help the head interface board firmware to detect any transmission errors.

The received data from the head interface board are cooling system feedback values monitored at the system's x-ray head, as well as the reported values of other x-ray head peripherals such as the state of the buttons, and the values from the magnetometers and collimator. All values are reported in 4-byte fields separated by delimiters. As the collimator ID value is a 64-bit value, there are two 4byte fields which together represent the 64-bit ID.
<<<

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWMC-295
TITLE: HVPS Interface Board Data
STATEMENT: >>>
The transmitted data to the HVPS interface board are commands to set various output control parameters including grid voltage, filament current, high voltage, and output power. There are also commands to initiate the process to test the high voltage and grid interlocks that the main control board provides to the HVPS interlock board.

The data received from the HVPS interface board are the target setpoint values for, as well as the monitored feedback values from, the HVPS. Additionally, status flags and IO values are reported from the HVPS interface board.
<<<
